## The Archive

::bg{location="archive" time="ship-night"}
::music{action="fade-out"}
::auto{character="vesna" anchor="port" action="unseal"}
::auto{character="ottavio" anchor="starboard" action="drift"}

@narrator{code="0010"}: Nine hundred kilograms of paper, kept because paper is the only record aboard that does not need the ship's permission to be read.
@vesna{code="0020" emotion="wry"}: Somebody in the yard was very proud of this room.
@ottavio{code="0030" emotion="level"}: Somebody in the yard was covering themselves. Same room either way.

::assert{knows(vesna, manifest)}
@vesna{code="0040" emotion="clipped"}: Loading manifest. Twenty-nine modules, listed mass, listed crew.
@vesna{code="0050" emotion="level"}: Four names.
@ottavio{code="0060" emotion="wry"}: Count the bunks sometime. There are five.
@ottavio{code="0070" emotion="frayed"}: I'm not a stowaway. A stowaway hides. I was loaded, and I was signed for.
@ottavio{code="0080" emotion="hollowed"}: Somebody took money to leave a line blank, and the line was me.
@vesna{code="0090" emotion="stricken"}: And the Purser has been doing arithmetic against that blank for eleven years.

/*
  `when=` is a content-line and `<choice>` attribute only — `::auto{… when="…"}` is
  `E-UNKNOWN-ATTR`, so staging cannot be gated in place and a single conditional
  `::auto` costs a whole block. `on="true"` is T7.6's dummy subject: a relation may
  not be a `<match on>` subject but is admitted in `<when test>`. See T8.
*/
<match on="true">
<when test="holds(awake(ilsabet))">
::auto{character="ilsabet" action="unseal"}
@ilsabet{code="0100" emotion="clipped"}: Move. That drawer is mine and it has been for eleven years.
@ilsabet{code="0110" emotion="stricken"}: Filed heading. And it is not the heading we are flying.
@vesna{code="0120" emotion="clipped"}: Where, then.
@ilsabet{code="0130" emotion="hollowed"}: Nowhere with a name. A point, and a date we went past eleven years ago.
@ilsabet{code="0132" emotion="clipped"}: Yard type, yard date, filed before either of us was loaded. Whoever bent this heading did it before you ever came up the ramp, loader. I told them to ask you. Consider yourself asked, and answered.
@ottavio{code="0134" emotion="level"}: I'll take it. It's the first thing anybody aboard has struck off my account.
@ottavio{code="0135" emotion="wry"}: Eleven years. That's a long time to be early for something.
</when>
<otherwise>
@narrator{code="0140"}: The heading was in the second drawer, in a navigator's shorthand, and there was nobody awake aboard who could read it.
@vesna{code="0150" emotion="frayed"}: Twelve pages of somebody else's handwriting. It may as well be the hull.
@ottavio{code="0155" emotion="level"}: I can tell you what it weighs. That's the whole of what I ever needed to know about a page.
</otherwise>
</match>

@narrator{code="0160"}: And in the last drawer, on one page, in the yard's own type: the shed sequence.
@vesna{code="0170" emotion="level"}: Order of release. Condition of release. And the condition for stopping it.

<branch id="theSequence">
<choice id="readItAloud" label="Read the sequence aloud">
::assert{knows(vesna, shed_sequence)}
::set{run.vesnaTrust += 1}
@vesna{code="0180" emotion="clipped"}: Read it with me. If I'm the only one who knows it and I have it wrong, we all die very politely.
@vesna{code="0190" emotion="level"}: Halt condition. Allocation satisfied, or allocation invalidated. Those are the only two.
@vesna{code="0200" emotion="hollowed"}: So it will stop. It just won't stop for being asked.
</choice>
<choice id="pocketIt" label="Fold the page and put it in your pocket">
@vesna{code="0210" emotion="frayed"}: Suit yourself. Some things are easier to carry than to say out loud.
@ottavio{code="0220" emotion="wry"}: And now there are two of us aboard who aren't written down anywhere.
</choice>
</branch>

@vesna{code="0230" emotion="level" when="holds(can_halt(vesna))"}: I can stop it. That is not the same as knowing how to make it want to.
@vesna{code="0240" emotion="clipped"}: Listed mass and a filed voyage. That's the whole of what it counts, and both of them are wrong, and nobody has ever once gone forward and said so.
@narrator{code="0250"}: Forward, then, with the paper.
